# Problems on Ages: Ratios, Differences, and Averages

> "Father is three times as old as his son; in ten years he will be twice as old."
> Age questions look like riddles, but each one is a pair of simple equations
> hiding behind a story. SSC CGL, CHSL, HSSC CET and police clerk papers set one
> or two of them almost every year because they test whether you can translate
> words into algebra quickly and calmly.

## 1. The Three Facts Behind Every Age Problem

[definition]
An **age problem** gives relationships between the ages of two or more people at
different moments (past, present, future) and asks for an age at one moment.
Every such problem rests on three facts: ages move together year by year, the
difference between two people's ages never changes, and ratios do change.
[/definition]

| Fact | Meaning | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Everyone ages equally | After n years add n to each person's age | 5 years later: 12 becomes 17 |
| The difference is fixed | A is 8 years older than B today, 10 years ago and 10 years hence | 30 and 22; 20 and 12 |
| The ratio is NOT fixed | Only the ratio at the stated moment is given | 3 : 2 today, 4 : 3 later |
| Sum of n people | The sum rises by n for every year that passes | Sum of 3 people: +3 per year |

The commonest error is adding the years to the *ratio* instead of to the *ages*.
Ratios are only a way to describe ages, so convert them to units first.

## 2. Setting Up with Ratio Units

If the present ages are in the ratio 3 : 5, write them as 3x and 5x. The
unknown x is the "value of one unit". Then any statement about the past or
future becomes an equation in x.

**Pattern A: ratio now, ratio later.** Present ratio 4 : 5; after 6 years it
becomes 5 : 6. Write (4x + 6) : (5x + 6) = 5 : 6. Cross-multiplying,
24x + 36 = 25x + 30, so x = 6 and the ages are 24 and 30.

**Pattern B: ratio now, ratio earlier.** Treat it the same way but subtract the
years from each age. Ten years ago A was half of B and the present ratio is
3 : 4: (3x - 10) = half of (4x - 10), which gives x = 5.

**Pattern C: single relation.** "Father is 4 times the son and the sum is 55"
gives 5 units = 55, so one unit (the son) is 11.

## 3. Using the Constant Difference

When the question gives or hides the age *difference*, a person-to-person
shortcut avoids a long equation.

[important]
Difference between two ages never changes. If the ratio at some moment is a : b,
then the difference of a - b units equals the known age gap. This fixes the
value of one unit in a single step.
[/important]

**Worked example.** A father is 30 years older than his son. After 5 years he
will be 3 times as old. At that time the gap of 30 years equals (3 - 1) = 2
units, so one unit (the son's age after 5 years) is 15. The son is 10 today.

Use the same idea for "years ago" problems: find the unit from the gap at the
moment where the ratio is given, then move back or forward.

## 4. Sums, Averages and Groups

| Situation | Method |
| --- | --- |
| Sum of ages is given for n people | Sum after t years = sum now + n x t |
| Average age of a group | Total age = average x number of people |
| A member joins | New member's age = new total - old total |
| A member leaves | Leaver's age = old total - new total |
| Replacement | Difference of total = new person - old person |
| Family average a few years ago | Add (years x members) to get the present total |
| Children born at equal gaps | Ages form a sequence x, x + d, x + 2d and so on |

**Teacher joins a class.** The average age of 30 students is 15. With the
teacher added the average becomes 16. The new total is 31 x 16 = 496 and the old
total was 450, so the teacher is 46. The teacher's age is the new total minus the
old total, never the new average.

**Family problems.** Total of three people 3 years ago plus 9 gives the total
today. A child who was born less than 3 years ago was not counted in the old
average, so read the dates carefully.

## 5. Typical SSC Patterns and Shortcuts

- **Ratio shortcut for n years later.** If the present ages are a and b and the
  ratio after n years is p : q, then the equation (a + n) q = (b + n) p solves
  the unknown directly.
- **Times-as-old formula.** If A is m times B now and k years ago A was r times
  B, then B today = k(r - 1) / (m - r). Check: father 3 times son, 8 years ago 7
  times: 8 x 6 / 4 = 12.
- **Birth-year trick.** Age = current year - birth year, so a question about
  "born in" years is the same difference idea.
- **Fractions of age.** "Mother's age is 7/3 of the daughter's" means ratio
  7 : 3, so take 7 units and 3 units.
- **Answer-checking.** Plug each option into the first condition. A wrong option
  usually breaks it within seconds, which is quick in MCQ papers.

## 6. Commonly Confused Points

| Trap | Correct approach |
| --- | --- |
| Adding years to the ratio | Add years to each actual age, not to the ratio terms |
| Forgetting which person the question asks about | Re-read the last line before choosing |
| "Years ago" treated as future | Years ago means subtract |
| Average after a member leaves | Use totals, not averages |
| Sum for n people after t years | Add n x t, not just t |

[example]
Ratio of the present ages of two brothers is 5 : 7. Eight years later it will be
3 : 4. Find the sum of their present ages.

Method. (5x + 8) : (7x + 8) = 3 : 4 gives 20x + 32 = 21x + 24, so x = 8. The
ages are 40 and 56 and the sum is 96. Check: after 8 years 48 : 64 = 3 : 4.
[/example]

[summary]
Convert every ratio into units (a x and b x), add or subtract the same number
of years from each real age, and keep the unit gap equal to the fixed age
difference. For groups use totals: total = average x members, and the sum rises
by n for every year. Plug options back into the first condition to confirm. The
difference between two people never changes; ratios and sums always do.
[/summary]
